{{also|Sing|Sing.|sing|sing.|siŋ|sîng|śing|ṣing}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sengʷʰ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|singen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|singan}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*singwan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*singwaną}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*sengʷʰ-}}.  Cognate with {{cog|de|singen||to sing}}.
{{listen|Česká státní hymna v G.ogg|Recorded '''singing''' from a person.}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|sĭng}}, {{IPA|en|/sɪŋ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-uk-sing.ogg|Audio (UK)}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-sing.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪŋ|s=1}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|sings|singing|sang|sung|past_ptc2=sungen|past_ptc2_qual=archaic}}<!--Sang or sung for preterite, according to AHD.-->

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[produce]] [[musical]] or [[harmonious]] [[sound]]s with one’s [[voice]].
#: {{ux|en|"I really want to '''sing''' in the school choir," said Vera.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To perform a [[vocal]] part in a musical composition, regardless of technique.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[express]] [[audibly]] by means of a harmonious [[vocalization]].
#: {{ux|en|'''sing''' a lullaby}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1852|author=Mrs M.A. Thompson|chapter=The Tutor's Daughter|title= Graham's American Monthly Magazine of Literature, Art, and Fashion| page=266| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=hdcRAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA266| passage=In the lightness of my heart I '''sang''' catches of songs as my horse gayly bore me along the well-remembered road.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[soothe]] with singing.
#: {{ux|en|to '''sing''' somebody to sleep}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} Of birds, to [[vocalise]]:
## {{lb|en|ornithology}} To produce a '[[song]]', for the purposes of [[defend]]ing a [[breeding]] [[territory]] or to attract a [[mate]].
## {{lb|en|literary}} To produce any type of [[melodious]] vocalisation.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Brækstad|author=w:Peter Christen Asbjørnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=68|passage=The evening was still very warm, and the birds in the woods were '''singing''' in praise of spring.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|slang}} To [[confess]] under [[interrogation]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To make a small, shrill sound.
#: {{ux|en|The air '''sings''' in passing through a crevice.}}
#: {{ux|en|a '''singing''' kettle}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Iliad|book=XXII|passage=O'er his head the flying spear / '''Sang''' innocent, and spent its force in air.}}
# To relate in verse; to celebrate in poetry.
#* {{RQ:Prior Solomon|page=468|passage=Again I bid the mournful Goddeſs write / The fond Purſuit of fugitive Delight: / Bid her exalt her melancholy Wing, / And rais'd from Earth, and ſav'd from Paſſion, '''ſing''' / Of human Hope by croſs Event deſtroyed, / Of uſeleſs Wealth, and Greatneſs unenjoy'd, {{...}}}}
#*{{RQ:Milton Poems|poem=Lycidas|passage=Who would not '''sing''' for Lycidas? he knew Himself to sing, and build the lofty rhyme.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To display [[fine]] qualities; to stand out as [[excellent]].
#: {{ux|en|The sauce really makes this lamb '''sing'''.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.news.ucsb.edu/2022/020678/out-starting-gate|work=The Current|passage=[Alissa Monte said] “This result was all about demonstrating that LZ [the LUX-ZEPLIN experiment] works, and it does! As we take more data and mature our analyses, we get to make LZ '''sing'''. {{...}}”|title=‘Out of the Starting Gate’|date=2022-07-07|author=Sonia Fernandez|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220707173606/https://www.news.ucsb.edu/2022/020678/out-starting-gate|archivedate=2022-07-07|accessdate=2022-07-20|publisher=University of California, Santa Barbara}}
# {{lb|en|ergative}} To be capable of being sung; to produce a certain effect by being sung.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1875|title=Blackwood's Edinburgh Magazine|volume=118|page=685
|passage=No song '''sings''' well unless it is open-vowelled, and has the rhythmic stress on the vowels. Tennyson's songs, for instance, are not generally adapted to music.}}
# {{lb|en|AU}} In traditional Aboriginal culture, to direct a [[supernatural]] influence on (a person or thing), usually malign; to [[curse]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author={{w|Alex Miller (writer)|Alex Miller}}|title=Journey to the Stone Country|publisher=Allen & Unwin|year_published=2003|page=343
|passage=‘We '''sung''' them two real good. We never give Louis Beck no place to find rest from his torment.’}}

